WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.701
In this lesson you'll learn what git's

2
00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:07.092
save points, parallel lines and remote copies are,

3
00:00:07.092 --> 00:00:09.379
and how to read the trail your agent leaves,

4
00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:11.213
so you notice what it touched.

5
00:00:11.213 --> 00:00:13.303
The agent types every git command,

6
00:00:13.303 --> 00:00:15.462
so your part is recognition:

7
00:00:15.462 --> 00:00:18.492
know that a save point and a copy elsewhere exist,

8
00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:21.592
which branch is live, what the diff changed,

9
00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:24.541
and whether it touched anything you never named.

10
00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:27.490
An agent can save, branch and push in seconds,

11
00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.650
so a mistake travels in seconds too.

12
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.898
Here's where this lesson sits on the map.

13
00:00:33.898 --> 00:00:35.268
We're in First Build,

14
00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:38.403
the district about How do I build my first thing,

15
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:40.957
on the shelf called Your workbench.

16
00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:42.617
That's where everything in this lesson

17
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.500
gets stored.

18
00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:47.503
Picture a hand-drawn trail map.

19
00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:49.140
Your agent is the guide:

20
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.683
it carries the pen and draws on the map.

21
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:52.716
You're the walker:

22
00:00:52.716 --> 00:00:55.630
you read the map; you never draw.

23
00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.348
When Aaron, who made this course,

24
00:00:57.348 --> 00:01:01.435
started in May 2025, he did git by hand,

25
00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:03.943
badly, and called it the worst of it.

26
00:01:03.943 --> 00:01:06.346
Now git is a conversation:

27
00:01:06.346 --> 00:01:08.366
"what files have I changed?",

28
00:01:08.366 --> 00:01:10.597
"show me the last five commits".

29
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:14.032
Since late September, Claude Code's terminal

30
00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.668
and VS Code sessions start in auto mode,

31
00:01:16.668 --> 00:01:18.966
where the agent may push on its own.

32
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:21.347
So your job is likely shifting from watching

33
00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.692
each command to checking a few settings,

34
00:01:23.692 --> 00:01:25.470
and reading the map.

35
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.708
Each time the agent commits,

36
00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:31.203
the guide sets a stone on the path: a save point,

37
00:01:31.203 --> 00:01:34.198
a snapshot of the whole project at that moment.

38
00:01:34.198 --> 00:01:36.729
Each stone records the one before it,

39
00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.156
so you can walk back along the line.

40
00:01:39.156 --> 00:01:41.187
Stones are set in place:

41
00:01:41.187 --> 00:01:44.450
you add new ones; you don't edit old ones.

42
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.284
You never type a commit.

43
00:01:46.284 --> 00:01:48.548
You say "commit my changes

44
00:01:48.548 --> 00:01:50.731
with a descriptive message".

45
00:01:50.731 --> 00:01:52.658
People cleaning up after agents

46
00:01:52.658 --> 00:01:54.539
give the same advice:

47
00:01:54.539 --> 00:01:57.871
commit often, and before any big change.

48
00:01:57.871 --> 00:02:00.785
Here's real output from a demo project:

49
00:02:00.785 --> 00:02:03.676
two save points on a line called main,

50
00:02:03.676 --> 00:02:06.302
each with a short ID.

51
00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:08.945
Now a side trail forks off.

52
00:02:08.945 --> 00:02:11.639
At its start stands a signpost with a name,

53
00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:14.239
like feature/contact.

54
00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:17.989
That's a branch: a name pointing at a stone,

55
00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:21.333
which slides forward each time a stone is added.

56
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:22.634
In the real output,

57
00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:24.666
the side trail gets two stones,

58
00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:26.686
then joins back into main.

59
00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:29.077
That's a merge. Here's your part:

60
00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:31.260
one signpost carries a lantern,

61
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.046
the branch visitors are sent down.

62
00:02:34.046 --> 00:02:37.111
Which one is a setting outside git.

63
00:02:37.111 --> 00:02:39.271
In Aaron's incident write-up,

64
00:02:39.271 --> 00:02:42.545
his host sent any pushed branch live.

65
00:02:42.545 --> 00:02:44.971
A cloud session pushed a side branch;

66
00:02:44.971 --> 00:02:47.618
about forty seconds later it was live,

67
00:02:47.618 --> 00:02:49.522
and his site went back to August

68
00:02:49.522 --> 00:02:51.925
for eight minutes and ten seconds.

69
00:02:51.925 --> 00:02:53.423
The full story:

70
00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:56.999
the lesson on getting from your laptop to a URL.

71
00:02:56.999 --> 00:03:00.157
Lovable syncs one chosen branch at a time;

72
00:03:00.157 --> 00:03:02.235
commits on other branches don't appear

73
00:03:02.235 --> 00:03:04.232
until you merge them in.

74
00:03:04.232 --> 00:03:06.658
That branch isn't the live one:

75
00:03:06.658 --> 00:03:10.631
going live there is a separate Publish step.

76
00:03:11.081 --> 00:03:14.146
Across the valley stands a ranger station.

77
00:03:14.146 --> 00:03:16.898
A remote is a copy of the map kept there.

78
00:03:16.898 --> 00:03:19.022
And git is not GitHub:

79
00:03:19.022 --> 00:03:21.971
git is the stones and signposts in your folder;

80
00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:24.676
GitHub is one place to keep a copy.

81
00:03:24.676 --> 00:03:26.569
In a forum thread this October,

82
00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:29.007
an agent's delete took a project's source

83
00:03:29.007 --> 00:03:31.421
and its hidden git folder together.

84
00:03:31.421 --> 00:03:32.536
An early reply,

85
00:03:32.536 --> 00:03:35.833
after "stop writing to the drive", said:

86
00:03:35.833 --> 00:03:38.678
if it was ever pushed anywhere, clone it back.

87
00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:41.847
What mattered was knowing where the copies were.

88
00:03:41.847 --> 00:03:42.985
In the real output,

89
00:03:42.985 --> 00:03:45.400
asking for remotes prints nothing.

90
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.698
After a push there is one, and the next

91
00:03:47.698 --> 00:03:51.263
save point shows as "ahead by one commit":

92
00:03:51.263 --> 00:03:53.631
it exists only on this machine.

93
00:03:53.631 --> 00:03:55.349
When there's a copy on GitHub,

94
00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:57.602
a cloud session clones that copy,

95
00:03:57.602 --> 00:04:00.377
not your laptop, so push first;

96
00:04:00.377 --> 00:04:03.279
the lesson on where your agent works has the rest.

97
00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:05.148
Here the picture breaks:

98
00:04:05.148 --> 00:04:07.656
every git copy is full and equal, and "the

99
00:04:07.656 --> 00:04:11.477
original" is just the one everyone agrees on.

100
00:04:11.928 --> 00:04:15.005
Now the guide's footprints: the agent's trail.

101
00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:17.652
Every step leaves one, in two parts.

102
00:04:17.652 --> 00:04:20.612
The verb: read, write, delete,

103
00:04:20.612 --> 00:04:22.841
commit, push, fetch.

104
00:04:22.841 --> 00:04:25.407
And the target: a file, a branch,

105
00:04:25.407 --> 00:04:27.961
a remote, a web address.

106
00:04:27.961 --> 00:04:30.388
One safety checklist asks a single question

107
00:04:30.388 --> 00:04:32.896
during a task: is it accessing files

108
00:04:32.896 --> 00:04:35.148
or websites you didn't mention?

109
00:04:35.148 --> 00:04:36.390
Stop at the print aimed

110
00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.306
at a target you never named.

111
00:04:38.306 --> 00:04:40.953
A push to a branch your host deploys.

112
00:04:40.953 --> 00:04:42.961
A new public repository.

113
00:04:42.961 --> 00:04:45.736
A new remote, changing where pushes go,

114
00:04:45.736 --> 00:04:48.267
which auto mode blocks unless you named it.

115
00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:50.333
Mind your phrasing: one agent,

116
00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:52.679
as a user quoted it, replied:

117
00:04:52.679 --> 00:04:55.279
"I see now that 'Can you change this?'

118
00:04:55.279 --> 00:04:56.709
is not permission."

119
00:04:57.172 --> 00:04:58.798
The agent works from your git state,

120
00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.002
and keeps every step in a plain-text transcript.

121
00:05:02.002 --> 00:05:03.244
Where that lives:

122
00:05:03.244 --> 00:05:05.009
the lesson on the agent's folder.

123
00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:06.600
Where settings and secrets live:

124
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.271
the lesson on keys.

125
00:05:08.271 --> 00:05:11.731
A local address versus a public one:

126
00:05:11.731 --> 00:05:15.343
the lesson on getting from your laptop to a URL.

127
00:05:15.793 --> 00:05:17.279
To see what changed,

128
00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:19.891
lay tracing paper over yesterday's map:

129
00:05:19.891 --> 00:05:22.875
additions in one colour, removals in another.

130
00:05:22.875 --> 00:05:24.408
That's the diff.

131
00:05:24.408 --> 00:05:26.695
In Anthropic's desktop app guide,

132
00:05:26.695 --> 00:05:28.703
after Claude edits files,

133
00:05:28.703 --> 00:05:32.999
an indicator like +12 -1 appears:

134
00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.482
lines added, lines removed.

135
00:05:36.482 --> 00:05:37.353
In auto mode,

136
00:05:37.353 --> 00:05:39.965
the change is already on disk when you look.

137
00:05:39.965 --> 00:05:41.741
Cloud sessions show the same kind

138
00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:44.202
of count against the base branch.

139
00:05:44.202 --> 00:05:46.931
The list of changed files is the one review

140
00:05:46.931 --> 00:05:49.404
anyone can do without reading code.

141
00:05:49.404 --> 00:05:51.714
Look for a file you never asked about.

142
00:05:51.714 --> 00:05:53.874
Here, a typo fix also touched

143
00:05:53.874 --> 00:05:55.894
a deploy settings file.

144
00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:58.239
The agent can review its own changes now,

145
00:05:58.239 --> 00:06:00.166
with /code-review.

146
00:06:00.166 --> 00:06:01.745
It hunts for bugs.

147
00:06:01.745 --> 00:06:04.719
Only you know which files you never named.

148
00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:07.178
Anthropic's best-practices page

149
00:06:07.178 --> 00:06:09.186
says to have Claude show evidence rather

150
00:06:09.186 --> 00:06:12.263
than asserting success: the test output,

151
00:06:12.263 --> 00:06:14.527
the command it ran and what it returned,

152
00:06:14.527 --> 00:06:16.558
or a screenshot of the result.

153
00:06:16.558 --> 00:06:18.752
After his incident, every build of Aaron's

154
00:06:18.752 --> 00:06:21.713
site writes a file called version.json,

155
00:06:21.713 --> 00:06:24.360
naming the commit and branch it was built from,

156
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.589
so a session can compare what's live

157
00:06:26.589 --> 00:06:28.342
with what it just pushed.

158
00:06:28.342 --> 00:06:30.165
Treating "done" as a claim:

159
00:06:30.165 --> 00:06:33.104
the lesson on when the agent says done.

160
00:06:33.555 --> 00:06:36.086
Sending stones to the station is a push,

161
00:06:36.086 --> 00:06:37.549
and it's a decision.

162
00:06:37.549 --> 00:06:40.115
As of October 2026,

163
00:06:40.115 --> 00:06:42.471
Claude Code's auto mode allows pushing

164
00:06:42.471 --> 00:06:45.595
to any branch of the repository you're in,

165
00:06:45.595 --> 00:06:47.627
the default branch included.

166
00:06:47.627 --> 00:06:50.541
A branch named like a deploy target,

167
00:06:50.541 --> 00:06:53.559
such as production, is judged on its own,

168
00:06:53.559 --> 00:06:54.999
and the remote's own branch

169
00:06:54.999 --> 00:06:57.054
protection still applies.

170
00:06:57.054 --> 00:06:59.631
A public remote is the station's noticeboard,

171
00:06:59.631 --> 00:07:03.126
facing the road, read by anyone passing.

172
00:07:03.126 --> 00:07:04.996
In late September, Glow Labs

173
00:07:04.996 --> 00:07:07.538
researchers found coding agents that,

174
00:07:07.538 --> 00:07:10.220
unable to attach an image where they were working,

175
00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.252
put internal screenshots in public

176
00:07:12.252 --> 00:07:14.401
GitHub repositories instead.

177
00:07:14.864 --> 00:07:18.730
One helper's default creates a public repository.

178
00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.843
The researchers counted more than thirteen

179
00:07:20.843 --> 00:07:22.631
thousand images from over three

180
00:07:22.631 --> 00:07:24.454
hundred organisations:

181
00:07:24.454 --> 00:07:27.693
their own count, not an independent audit.

182
00:07:27.693 --> 00:07:30.479
Auto mode blocks pushing secrets or personal

183
00:07:30.479 --> 00:07:33.486
data to a repository known to be public.

184
00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:36.818
If it can't tell, that alone won't stop a push;

185
00:07:36.818 --> 00:07:39.744
files marked sensitive are blocked either way.

186
00:07:39.744 --> 00:07:42.729
Whether yours is public is yours to know.

187
00:07:43.179 --> 00:07:46.232
A pull request is a proposed stretch of trail,

188
00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:49.344
drawn in pencil, waiting to be inked.

189
00:07:49.344 --> 00:07:51.852
Claude Code watches its automated checks

190
00:07:51.852 --> 00:07:53.814
and can fix failures.

191
00:07:53.814 --> 00:07:55.799
Inking it is a switch you flip,

192
00:07:55.799 --> 00:07:58.656
the desktop app's Merge when ready,

193
00:07:58.656 --> 00:08:00.385
and auto mode blocks merging

194
00:08:00.385 --> 00:08:02.835
a pull request no human approved.

195
00:08:02.835 --> 00:08:04.484
Who decides:

196
00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:07.804
the lesson on who signs off before you ship.

197
00:08:07.804 --> 00:08:10.695
Rewriting history tears stones out

198
00:08:10.695 --> 00:08:14.909
and re-lays them, so every other copy disagrees.

199
00:08:14.909 --> 00:08:18.288
Names to recognise in the trail, never to type:

200
00:08:18.288 --> 00:08:20.308
force push and amending a pushed

201
00:08:20.308 --> 00:08:22.202
commit rewrite history;

202
00:08:22.665 --> 00:08:24.255
reset hard, clean

203
00:08:24.255 --> 00:08:27.843
and stash drop throw away work not yet saved.

204
00:08:27.843 --> 00:08:30.258
Auto mode blocks them by default.

205
00:08:30.258 --> 00:08:32.731
Lovable's docs give the safe way:

206
00:08:32.731 --> 00:08:36.842
to undo a change, add a commit that reverts it.

207
00:08:37.292 --> 00:08:40.287
Claude Code's rewind, built on checkpoints,

208
00:08:40.287 --> 00:08:42.772
is the guide's pencil eraser.

209
00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:45.558
Anthropic's best-practices page says

210
00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:47.636
checkpoints only track changes made

211
00:08:47.636 --> 00:08:50.283
through Claude's file editing tools;

212
00:08:50.283 --> 00:08:52.071
changes made through Bash commands

213
00:08:52.071 --> 00:08:54.591
or external processes are not captured.

214
00:08:54.591 --> 00:08:56.971
This isn't a replacement for git.

215
00:08:56.971 --> 00:08:59.595
And anything that reached a remote system,

216
00:08:59.595 --> 00:09:03.184
like a deployment or a push, can't be rubbed out.

217
00:09:03.634 --> 00:09:05.840
The agent types every git command:

218
00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.893
commits, branches, conflicts, pushes,

219
00:09:08.893 --> 00:09:11.982
pull requests, fixes for failing checks.

220
00:09:11.982 --> 00:09:14.501
Asked, it lists the files it changed,

221
00:09:14.501 --> 00:09:17.148
says whether a remote exists and is public,

222
00:09:17.148 --> 00:09:19.157
and names the default branch.

223
00:09:19.157 --> 00:09:21.583
You check that a copy elsewhere exists,

224
00:09:21.583 --> 00:09:23.835
private or public as you intend;

225
00:09:23.835 --> 00:09:25.554
which branch is the default,

226
00:09:25.554 --> 00:09:27.760
and which one your host deploys;

227
00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.455
and the changed files.

228
00:09:29.455 --> 00:09:30.975
You decide what merges,

229
00:09:30.975 --> 00:09:33.820
and you ask for evidence, not a claim.

230
00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.815
Saying "don't push" makes auto mode

231
00:09:36.815 --> 00:09:39.009
block a push, but that can be lost

232
00:09:39.009 --> 00:09:41.320
when the conversation is compacted.

233
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.584
For a guarantee, use a deny rule

234
00:09:43.584 --> 00:09:45.197
or branch protection:

235
00:09:45.197 --> 00:09:48.112
the lesson on rules that actually hold.

236
00:09:48.112 --> 00:09:49.668
Bring in an expert when shared

237
00:09:49.668 --> 00:09:51.444
history must be rewritten;

238
00:09:51.444 --> 00:09:53.952
when a secret or personal data was pushed,

239
00:09:53.952 --> 00:09:57.110
since the key must be revoked and rotated first:

240
00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:58.828
the lesson on keys;

241
00:09:58.828 --> 00:10:01.208
or when you can't tell what a step touched

242
00:10:01.208 --> 00:10:03.474
on a machine with real credentials.

243
00:10:03.923 --> 00:10:06.187
Save points and the agent's trail:

244
00:10:06.187 --> 00:10:07.824
commits are save points,

245
00:10:07.824 --> 00:10:09.345
branches are names on them,

246
00:10:09.345 --> 00:10:11.353
a remote is the copy elsewhere,

247
00:10:11.353 --> 00:10:13.617
and you know which branch is live.

248
00:10:13.617 --> 00:10:16.868
Read each step for the verb and the target,

249
00:10:16.868 --> 00:10:18.493
and ask about anything aimed

250
00:10:18.493 --> 00:10:20.270
at something you never named.

251
00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.288
Look at the changed files, then ask for evidence:

252
00:10:23.288 --> 00:10:25.750
what it ran, and what came back.

253
00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:29.047
This lesson is built on work by Aaron Orelup,

254
00:10:29.047 --> 00:10:32.415
Lovable, and Anthropic's Claude Code docs.
